{{#tag:tabber
|Shadow Properties=
This is a list of general properties common for all Shadows. See also: [[#Shadow Quirks]].
*As the shadows appear, each one inflicts a small area knockdown which can only affect each enemy once.
*Nekros and his allies are able to walk through Shadows without being obstructed.
*Shadows have raised [[Threat Level]], causing enemies to prioritize attacking them over players.
*Shadows are able to use their original abilities, gear, and tactics (including [[Eximus]]).
**Shadows will take cover and organize, while occasionally seeking out to follow the player or the host around.
**Shadows will interact with the environment, for example using [[Rampart]]s, or hacking into disabled {{E|Ambulas}} or [[Bursa]] - turning them into allies just as if the player had hacked them.
**Shadows of enemies that leave explosions upon death will damage allies.
**Shadows of units such as {{E|Ancient Protector}} will '''not''' provide {{D|Overguard}}.
**Other buffing Shadows (e.g. {{E|Shield Osprey}}, {{E|Ancient Healer}}, {{E|Aerolyst}}) ''may'' affect [[Defense Objects]], [[Excavator]]s, [[Companions]], players, and allied NPCs.
***They will prioritize following your Shadows, or enemies if not in range of Shadows.
*Shadows are counted as summons and are affected by {{M|Summoner's Wrath}}, {{Arcane|Arcane Camisado}}, and {{Arcane|Theorem Infection}}.
**They are able to damage enemies immune to ability damage (e.g. {{E|Arbitration Shield Drone}}).
**Shadows retain summons buffs long after application, specifically from {{Arcane|Theorem Infection}}, without the need to snapshot the buffs on spawn.
**Shadow eximus abilities ''may'' receive elemental damage bonuses and ''may'' grant buffs to the player, for example: 
***[[Leech Eximus]] will create a damage zone which heals allies, it also deals {{D|Cold}} damage and status procs when applying {{Arcane|Theorem Infection}} with {{Arcane|Residual Malodor}}. 
***[[Shock Eximus]] will trigger {{M|Archon Stretch}}. Other elemental eximus ''may'' trigger other archon mods. [[Leech Eximus]] will '''not''' trigger {{M|Archon Intensify}}.
*[[Eximus]] Shadows possess [[Overguard]], which isn't considered as a part of their health and will '''not''' be drained, making them far more resilient than their normal variants.
*Enemies that spawn additional enemies (i.e. {{E|Drahk Master}}) can add to the number of Shadows, even above the max Shadow count.
*Kills by a player's Shadows grant that player '''50%''' of the kill experience to their Warframe.
*Shadows are black, and have a mist effect that is colored with Nekros' energy color. A pure black energy color will remove the mist effect and leave simply blackened forms.
*If a Shadow enters inside the sphere of a {{E|Nullifier Crewman}} or {{E|Corrupted Nullifier}}, or gets in contact with the disrupting aura of a [[Comba]] or [[Scrambus]], the shadow will be drained of health while remaining inside the bubble or aura. If Nekros is the one making contact with one of the mentioned, his Shadows will '''not''' die. <code>Only tested with {{E|Nullifier Crewman}} so far.</code>

{{!}}-{{!}}Summoning Priority=
*Shadow copies are taken from a list of the last '''20''' enemies killed by either Nekros or his Shadows; teammate, [[Companion]], and [[Operator]] mode kills do '''not''' count. Once a specific enemy has been copied, it is removed from the list.
**The game does '''not''' remember any kills past the 20th, for example: if you kill '''30''' enemies and cast this ability '''4''' times without killing any more enemies, this ability will create '''7''', '''7''', '''6''', and '''0''' Shadows.
**When using weapons with [[Syndicate]] mods, or weapons with built-in syndicate effects, the radial blast will count as the player's kills, which will overwrite any current ones.
*{{A|Soul Punch}} is the highest "priority" in a way, because it (or it's Mark) immediately summons a Shadow. 
**Some Shadows can be created '''only''' via {{A|Soul Punch}} instakill. Some enemies can'''not''' be copied.
*{{A|Shadows of the Dead}} summoning priority is such: '''1)''' the highest level enemies in the list; '''2)''' the order of appearance in the list (first to die); '''3)''' [[Eximus]], otherwise the highest priority group.
**[[Eximus]] variants will <u>ignore the priority group</u> and will '''only''' respect enemy levels and the order of appearance in the list. [[Eximus]] enemies spawn with higher levels than the rest, which means they are almost always prioritized.
**After some time, the killed enemies from the list ''may'' have lower levels compared to "fresh" incoming enemies.
*The <code>''?''</code> priority means the unit can definitely be copied, but it's hard to test the priority of it. 

